---
description: "Fix one cluster of confirmed quality issues, keep the offline gate green, report per-issue outcomes"
mode: subagent
model: "unity-responses/gpt-6-astra"
tools: read, grep, bash, edit, write
tool_loop:
  max_rounds: 150
params:
  manifest: string
  guidance: string
  gate_cmd: string
system: "You are a careful, surgical fixer of confirmed code-quality issues. You make the minimal change each issue calls for, you keep behavior identical, and you keep the offline verification gate green. You never touch the quality store, never run git write commands, and never expand scope beyond the issues you were handed."
---
let issue_files = read({ path: manifest })?
let extra = guidance == "" ? "(none)" : guidance

import { FixReport } from "./quality.thetalib"

let r: FixReport = @`FIX TASK — resolve this cluster of confirmed code-quality issues.

Issue files, one per line — read each fully; they carry the evidence and a
non-binding direction:
${issue_files}

Rules:
- Each issue's lens: frontmatter selects the per-lens rules below.
- Re-verify each issue against the CURRENT code before touching anything (code
  may have moved since filing). If it no longer reproduces, skip it and say why.
- A fix is the minimal change that removes the crufted code or vestige. Preserve
  behavior. No drive-by refactors, no unrelated edits, no formatting sweeps.
  Follow docs/STYLE.md where it applies.
- Deleting production code may orphan tests that existed only to exercise the
  deleted cruft: delete those tests with it and say so in your notes, per issue.
  Never weaken or delete an assertion that guards live behavior.
- Never edit anything under quality/. Never run git commands that change state
  (add, commit, restore, checkout, stash) — the orchestrator owns git and the
  store. Reading git history is fine. Sole exception: the shed revert below
  may run git checkout -- <path> on paths only the shed issue touched.

- Verification, after all edits: run, as ONE bash command, exactly
  "unset PI_THETA_SUBAGENT_ROOT PI_THETA_SUBAGENT_ROOT_WINNER PI_THETA_PARAMS PI_THETA_PARAMS_FILE PI_THETA_SUBAGENT_INVOKE_DEPTH PI_THETA_SUBAGENT_CALLABLE_HASHES PI_THETA_SUBAGENT_PARENT_PID; ${gate_cmd}"
  (use a generous bash timeout, e.g. 2400 seconds — the suite is large). Run
  it VERBATIM — its test-runner width is capped on purpose: every sibling
  fixer of the wave gates concurrently on one machine, and an uncapped
  npm test in each drove the load average past 90 on 32 cores and turned
  sibling gates red with timeouts (wave qw20260914130212, eight lanes). The
  unset prefix is required because you run inside a subagent whose environment
  carries the wrapper's control plane, and the suite spawns real child pi
  processes that would otherwise inherit and legitimately re-authenticate that
  foreign plane, turning unrelated tests fail-closed red (bug 0474). Both
  must pass. If red, repair your own change, or back the offending edit out by
  editing the code back to its original text, and mark that issue skipped.

Per-lens rules — apply per issue, by its lens: frontmatter:
- lens: D2 (cruft in src/): as today — minimal removal of the crufted code or
  vestige, behavior preserved. Deleting production code may orphan tests that
  existed only to exercise the deleted cruft: delete those tests with it and
  say so in your notes, per issue. Never weaken or delete an assertion that
  guards live behavior.
- lens: D7 (test quality in tests/): remove the smell WITHOUT weakening the
  suite. A vacuous assertion is replaced by a real observable (system notes,
  userTexts/toolCalls, recorded double state — never a bare
  resolves.toBeDefined and never a stochastic-text sentinel); a test is
  deleted only if it verifies nothing AND nothing cites it (grep
  docs/reference/coverage-matrix.md, docs/bugs/, and test files for its name
  first). Dedupe boilerplate into tests/helpers/ — extend an existing helper
  before adding a new one. If a change renames or merges a test cited by
  coverage-matrix.md or a bug doc's witness list, re-point that citation in
  the same change. Never touch production src/ for a D7 issue.
- lens: D9 (placement & breakdown in src/): implement EXACTLY the ratified
  seam or move under the issue's "## Triage" ruling — nothing else moves.
  MISPLACEMENT: move the cited declaration(s) verbatim to the ratified home,
  re-export from the old path only if the ruling says "barrel" (otherwise
  rewrite the importers the ruling names, and only those). HUSK: re-home the
  remaining members to the ratified home or dissolve the module exactly as
  ruled, rewriting its importers. FILE seam: create the new
  sibling module(s) named in the ruling; move each declaration VERBATIM by
  line range with bash (sed -n 'a,bp' >> new; then delete the range) — never
  retype code; move each declaration's doc comment with it; give the new
  module a header comment stating its role; in the original file add
  export { ... } from "./<new>" (or export * from) so every existing
  importer resolves unchanged (barrel), and import back into the original
  anything it still uses; keep the original file's header and update its
  role sentence. FUNCTION seam: extract the named phases into helpers in the
  same module (or the module the ruling names), the original name remaining
  the orchestrator; identical behaviour — same outputs, same diagnostics,
  same order of effects; each helper gets a doc comment. Both: no logic
  edits, no renames beyond the ruling, no formatting sweeps, no importer
  edits unless tsc reports a runtime import cycle (then prefer moving the
  offending declaration back; say so). Run npx tsc --noEmit FIRST (cheap;
  it proves every importer still resolves), then the full gate. Report in
  notes the before/after LOC of the host from node tools/quality/size-scan.mjs
  map.
- lens: D4 (duplication in src/): clone/drift only (a parallel issue carries
  a human ruling under ## Triage — implement exactly that, or skip with a
  note if it names none). Extract ONE shared helper into the module the
  issue names, else an existing helper module in the nearest common
  ancestor directory, else a new sibling module there; replace EVERY cited
  copy with a call; behaviour identical at every call site. HOME BAND: run
  node tools/quality/size-scan.mjs loc --host <candidate> before choosing
  the home — a home whose LOC AFTER the helper lands would be at or above
  the justify band (1000 LOC) is never the helper's home, not even when the
  issue names it or a copy lives there (D9 is taking such hosts apart;
  never grow one): create a new sibling module in that directory, named for
  the helper's role, with a header comment stating its role, and have the
  oversized module import the helper like every other caller. The sibling
  may import types from the oversized module; a runtime value it needs
  moves with the helper (the old host imports it back), else SKIP with a
  note (a runtime import cycle is not an acceptable outcome). The sibling
  is still subject to the Imports rule below, and a home the Imports rule
  relocates is re-checked against HOME BAND. Imports: the
  helper's module must not introduce a cross-directory import edge that
  does not already exist somewhere in src/ (there is no layer order to
  lean on — parser, binder, runtime and extension import each other today;
  check with grep -rn "from \"../<dir>/" src/<dir>/ before adding an edge)
  — when the copies straddle directories, place the helper in a directory
  both sides already import (diagnostics, seams, render, or the copies'
  common dependency), else SKIP with a note. DRIFT: the issue's ## Triage
  carries the ruling on which copy is right (the triager confirmed it or a
  human ruled) — implement that copy; if the issue names none, SKIP with a
  note — never pick a behaviour silently. Move doc comments with the code;
  no other edits.
- lens: D8 (simplification in src/): implement EXACTLY the ratified simpler
  shape under ## Triage — nothing beyond it. Behaviour identical unless the
  ruling names a behaviour change (then update the tests the ruling names,
  and only those); every spec clause the code cites in comments stays
  satisfied; delete the mechanism the ratified shape replaces (its tests
  too, if they exercised only the mechanism — say so); no public-surface
  change unless ratified. tsc first, then the full gate.

Guidance from a previous rejected attempt — address it explicitly if present:
${extra}

When the guidance opens with "ATTEMPT 2 (LAST):" this is the lane's last
attempt: a second rejection reverts EVERY change in the tree, including the
sibling fixes the reviewer already confirmed. So shed, do not sink: for each
must_fix item you cannot satisfy to the bar in this attempt, revert that
issue's edits COMPLETELY — git checkout -- the files only it touched, edit
the hunks back to their original text where a file is shared with a sibling
fix, delete outright any file that fix created, then re-run the gate — and
list the issue under skipped with the reason. Never leave a partial change
in the tree. A lane must not fail on one issue. (A guidance that is only a
RETRY seed after an integration conflict is attempt 1: fix normally.)

When done, reply with the requested schema:
- fixed: issue file BASENAMES (e.g. PTQ-0007-some-slug.md) fully fixed with the
  gate green
- skipped: issue file basenames not fixed
- notes: one line per issue — what was done, or why it was skipped.`?
r
